# Review fix (Important #3, Task 8): a real 301 for /blogs, not just the
# client-side <Navigate to="/blog" replace /> in App.tsx. Without this line,
# https://…/blogs answered 200 (the SPA shell, from the catch-all below) and
# only client-side JS moved the visitor -- so a non-rendering crawler
# following the sitemap's own /blogs entry (plugins/sitemap.ts) learned
# nothing, Search Console reported the submitted URL as a soft/JS redirect,
# and an inbound press link pointing at /blogs passed no redirect signal at
# all. This line is what actually makes plugins/sitemap.ts's own comment
# ("a crawler that follows it learns the new address") true.
#
# MUST sit above the catch-all: Cloudflare Pages processes _redirects
# top-to-bottom and stops at the first match, so a rule below `/* ...`
# would never be reached -- the catch-all already answers everything.
# src/test/hosting.test.ts pins both the rule and this ordering.
#
# The client-side <Navigate> in App.tsx STAYS, as the in-app fallback: Vite
# preview (`npm run preview`, what Task 10's Playwright suite drives against)
# does not process this file at all, so without it a Playwright /blogs test
# would see the SPA shell with no redirect ever happening.
/blogs  /blog  301

# Deliberately no /api/* rule here. Cloudflare Pages' _redirects only accepts
# status codes 200, 301, 302, 303, 307, or 308 -- an invalid status is silently
# ignored at build time, not rejected, so there is no way to make this file
# answer /api/* with a 404 or any other "this path does not belong to the SPA"
# response. A 200-rewrite would be worse than nothing: it would make an
# unrouted /api/* request look identical to a successful API response.
# The Worker route in wrangler.toml (`viabiancadelhi.com/api/*`) is therefore
# the *only* mechanism keeping /api/* out of the catch-all below -- Cloudflare
# Routes take precedence over Pages for a matching hostname, so as long as
# that route exists and is active, this catch-all never sees an /api/*
# request at all. src/test/hosting.test.ts pins the route's existence,
# pattern and zone instead of anything in this file. If you're adding a rule
# here for some other path, know that this file cannot express "fail loudly"
# for it -- see docs/cloudflare-cutover.md's deploy-time curl smoke check for
# the only reliable way to confirm the route is actually live.
/*    /index.html   200
